* {{rhymes|en|eɪd|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} A thick heavy [[fabric]] into which raised patterns have been [[woven]], originally in gold and silver; more recently any cloth [[incorporate|incorporating]] raised, woven patterns.<ref name= "isbn0-19-861271-0"> Brown, Lesley The New shorter Oxford English dictionary on historical principles. pub. Clarendon Oxford 1993 isbn=0-19-861271-0</ref>
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|pages=65–66|volume=I|passage=Madame Legarde, the "glass of fashion and the nurse of form," (alias the most fashionable of milliners,) has comfortably assured me, "that my figure has great merit, and only requires cultivation:" this is to be done by tissues, '''brocades''', and laces, which are now scattered round me in charming confusion.}}
#* {{RQ:Bellow Humboldt's Gift|year=1976|page=321|passage=… his desire to stand in '''brocade''' and sing Rhadames in ''Aida'' was like my eagerness to go far, far beyond fellow intellectuals of my generation who had lost the imaginative soul.}}
#* {{RQ:Wolfe_Urth|chapter=XXV|page=149|passage=I selected a pair of loose, dark trousers bound at the waist with a russet sash, a tunic with an open neck and large pockets, and a cloak of the true fuligin of that guild of which I am still officially a master, lined with particolored '''brocade'''.}}
# An item decorated with brocade.
# Any of several species of [[noctuid]] moths such as some [[species]] in the [[genera]] ''[[Wikipedia:Calophasia|Calophasia]]'' and ''[[Wikipedia:Hadena|Hadena]]''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=P.P. Mary; et al.|passage=Other species considered occasional migrants have become established in the UK in recent years, such as the ... sombre '''brocade''', Blair's mocha, Flame '''brocade''', and Clifden nonpareil.|editors=Akshay Kumar Chakravarthy; et al.|title=Arthropod Diversity and Conservation in the Tropics and Sub-tropics|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wLiVDQAAQBAJ|publisher=Springer|isbn=978-981-10-1518-2}}
# {{lb|en|metaphoric}} A decorative pattern.
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1826|title=A picturesque and topographical account of Cheltenham, and its Vicinity|author=Thomas Dudley Fosbroke |page=|ISBN=|passage=The shrubbery around the cottages is a '''brocade''' of lawns and shrubs intermixed, in fancy patterns, with gravel walks, in various directions, which wind into the woods.}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1976|title=Pain and Grace: A Study of Two Mystical Writers of Eighteenth-Century Muslim India|author=Annemarie Schimmel |page=126|ISBN=|passage=It is as though the poets and mystics were weaving a colorful '''brocade''' of words with the intention to please God and to show His greatness to the world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en |year=2012 |title=The Book of Tea |author=Kakuzo Okakura |page=36|ISBN=0486139999|passage=Saying this, Rikyu stepped into the garden, shook a tree and scattered over the garden gold and crimson leaves, scraps of the '''brocade''' of autumn!}}

